How to read this document {#how-to-read}
----------------------------------------

*This section is non-normative.*

[[#profiles]] defines what a deployment MUST implement to claim conformance, and which
capabilities are optional profiles on top of that baseline. [[#concepts]] carries the
cross-cutting definitions — agents, identity, session lifecycle, intent, capability, skill,
provenance, and trust basis — that the pillar sections reference rather than restate.

The three pillars follow. **Instrument** ([[#wire]] through [[#errors]]) specifies real-time
interception, evaluation, and enforcement: the wire format, the handshake, the hook
taxonomy, the disposition vocabulary, the SessionContext and Intent model, replay
protection, and signature semantics. **Trace** ([[#trace]]) specifies the event vocabulary.
**Inspect** ([[#inspect]]) specifies the AgBOM. [[#bindings]] covers how MCP and A2A traffic
travels through ACS.

A normative statement lives at the altitude of its scope. Cross-cutting invariants — those
true across pillars — are stated once in [[#concepts]]. Mechanism requirements — those true
within one pillar's enforcement — stay in that pillar.

The machine-readable schemas are normative alongside this prose and live under
<a href="https://github.com/afogel/ACS_official/tree/dev/specification/v0.1.0">`specification/v0.1.0/`</a>
[[ACS-SCHEMAS]]. Each section links the schema that governs the shape it describes; where
prose and schema disagree, that is a bug worth
<a href="https://github.com/afogel/ACS_official/issues">reporting</a>.
